<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>CFBundleDevelopmentRegion</key>
	<string>en</string>
	<key>CFBundleDisplayName</key>
        <string>Keep Contact</string>
	<key>CFBundleExecutable</key>
	<string>$(EXECUTABLE_NAME)</string>
	<key>CFBundleIdentifier</key>
	<string>$(PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER)</string>
	<key>CFBundleInfoDictionaryVersion</key>
	<string>6.0</string>
	<key>CFBundleName</key>
	<string>$(PRODUCT_NAME)</string>
	<key>CFBundlePackageType</key>
	<string>APPL</string>
	<key>CFBundleShortVersionString</key>
	<string>$(MARKETING_VERSION)</string>
	<key>CFBundleVersion</key>
	<string>$(CURRENT_PROJECT_VERSION)</string>
	<key>LSRequiresIPhoneOS</key>
	<true/>
	<key>UILaunchStoryboardName</key>
	<string>LaunchScreen</string>
	<key>UIMainStoryboardFile</key>
	<string>Main</string>
	<key>UIRequiredDeviceCapabilities</key>
	<array>
		<string>armv7</string>
	</array>
	<key>UISupportedInterfaceOrientations</key>
	<array>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortrait</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeLeft</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeRight</string>
	</array>
	<key>UISupportedInterfaceOrientations~ipad</key>
	<array>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortrait</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortraitUpsideDown</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeLeft</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeRight</string>
	</array>
	<key>UIViewControllerBasedStatusBarAppearance</key>
	<true/>
	<!-- Native auth deep link. Mirrors the Android manifest's
	     com.keepcontact.app://auth intent filter; without it the Supabase
	     callback cannot return into the app and sign-in never completes. -->
	<key>CFBundleURLTypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>CFBundleURLName</key>
			<string>com.keepcontact.app</string>
			<key>CFBundleURLSchemes</key>
			<array>
				<string>com.keepcontact.app</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
	<!-- Passive liveness evidence (KC-IOS-UNLOCK-SPIKE-001, ADR-0039).
	     remote-notification is the primary path: the server wakes the app with a
	     silent push and the app answers with the current lock state. A silent
	     push is best-effort — iOS may delay it, coalesce it, or not deliver it
	     at all, so it may not wake the app and cannot on its own prove that
	     anyone is being watched. There is no continuous location session; that
	     is what previously put an indicator in the status bar and drew a
	     battery baseline.
	     The background `location` mode was removed (human decision, 2026-08-10)
	     and stays removed. Declaring a location background mode the app does not
	     use for location is exactly the kind of claim App Review pushes back on,
	     and it made the app look more protective than it was.

	     Significant-change monitoring was restored on 2026-08-14 (human decision)
	     without it, because it never needed it: `UIBackgroundModes: location` is
	     required only for a continuous session via `startUpdatingLocation` +
	     `allowsBackgroundLocationUpdates`. `startMonitoringSignificantLocationChanges`
	     relaunches a terminated app on Always authorization alone. The 2026-08-10
	     removal took out both the unnecessary background mode and the necessary
	     Always usage description; only the second is coming back, so what the app
	     declares now matches exactly what it does.

	     It remains best-effort and remains a wake path, not evidence: it fires
	     only when the device moves between cell towers, so it does nothing for a
	     person who stays home, and no coordinate is ever read, stored, or sent.
	     Foreground location is separate and genuinely used by SOS. -->
	<key>UIBackgroundModes</key>
	<array>
		<string>remote-notification</string>
	</array>
	<key>NSLocationAlwaysAndWhenInUseUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Keep Contact uses this only to restart its guardian if the app gets closed, so the people you chose can still tell you are fine. It never reads, stores, or sends your location, and it does not track your movements.</string>
	<key>NSLocationWhenInUseUsageDescription</key>
	<string>If you send an SOS, Keep Contact can attach your current location so the people you chose can find you. It is used only for that, only while the app is open, and it never tracks your movements.</string>
	<key>NSCameraUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Keep Contact 需要使用摄像头扫描二维码，以加入守护圈或同步登录凭据。</string>
	<!-- HealthKit wakes KC and permits a bounded positive-history query. Only a
	     positive step/floor result and its interval time become normalized
	     evidence; counts and raw samples stay on-device. A wake or zero result is
	     never a check-in. Read-only, so KC never writes Health data. -->
	<!-- CoreMotion: step history, activity type and the brief accelerometer
	     sample that tells a phone in a hand from one lying on a table. Missing
	     this string is a hard crash the moment CMPedometer is touched, not a
	     silent refusal. -->
	<key>NSMotionUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Keep Contact reads your phone's own step and movement history so your circle can tell you are fine without you opening the app. It never records where you go, and no movement data leaves your phone beyond a count.</string>
	<key>NSHealthShareUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Keep Contact checks that your phone has recorded recent movement, so your circle can tell you are fine without you having to open the app. Only the fact that you were active and the time is used — no health data ever leaves your phone.</string>
	<!-- KC requests `toShare: []` and never writes health data. This string is
	     here because App Store upload validation keys off the *entitlement*, not
	     off which APIs are actually called: a build carrying the HealthKit
	     entitlement is rejected without both strings even when the write half is
	     never used. Omitting it cost a whole TestFlight build (run 30833103571,
	     altool error 90683). The wording says plainly that nothing is written,
	     rather than inventing a purpose that does not exist. -->
	<key>NSHealthUpdateUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Keep Contact never writes anything to Health. It only reads whether your phone recorded recent movement, so your circle can tell you are fine without you having to open the app.</string>
	<!-- No custom cryptography; keeps TestFlight builds from stalling on the
	     export-compliance question at every upload. -->
	<key>ITSAppUsesNonExemptEncryption</key>
	<false/>
</dict>
</plist>
